dbHarness proposes to explore how Deutsche Bank can leverage its existing GPU infrastructure to run powerful open-weight AI models and make them directly useful to developers through an internally maintained agent harness.

We will begin with a concrete use case: deploy a capable open-weight coding model in collaboration with dbLLM and connect it to an open-source agent harness such as Pi or OpenCode. The resulting coding agent will be evaluated on end-to-end software engineering tasks, including investigating bugs, implementing fixes, preparing test data, running tests in isolated environments, and generating draft pull requests.

We will compare task accuracy, latency, developer effort, and total cost against existing Gemini CLI and Copilot-assisted workflows. A hypothetical Claude Code subscription scenario will provide an additional commercial cost reference. The objective is to establish whether an internally hosted agent can deliver competitive performance and favorable economics at scale.

Our larger ambition is to make open-weight models a readily accessible and routinely considered option across Deutsche Bank. Building on the existing capabilities of dbLLM and Ada, the same approach could enable wider adoption of internally hosted coding, OCR, embedding, vision, and reasoning models across enterprise workflows.

This would strengthen the bank’s ability to develop and maintain AI capabilities internally, improve control over sensitive data processing, reduce unnecessary reliance on external AI services, and maximize the value of existing GPU investments.